// HW2 timing program, written by the course staff. Do not edit this file.
//
// Build: g++ -std=c++17 $(cat flags.txt) timing.cpp matmul.cpp -o timing
// Run:   ./timing <your 9-digit student ID>
//        ./timing <your 9-digit student ID> --tests-only   (only the public tests, a few seconds)
//
// First it runs the public tests: tricky cases (for example C is A), not timed, each in its own process.
// A test passes if C equals A·B of the values A and B had before the call.
// Then, for every size, it:
//   - makes random matrices A and B from your student ID (same ID, same matrices, on any computer),
//   - calls your matmul() several times, with new A and B each time, and times only the call,
//   - checks every call: for 3 random vectors x, C·x must match A·(B·x)
//     within relative error 1e-4 (error = ||C·x - A·(B·x)|| / ||A·(B·x)||, 2-norm),
//   - checks that matmul doesn't change A or B and uses one thread (child processes count too),
//   - measures peak memory (each size runs in its own process),
//   - if a call takes under 2 s, times the size in 3 rounds, each in a new process, and keeps its median round
//     (the one in the middle by cost). A size passes only if every round passes.
// Last, it prints a preview of your contest score, over 5 of these sizes, chosen to be like the contest's.
// It writes everything to results.csv in the folder you run it from (not with --tests-only).
// Every run overwrites results.csv, and also saves a numbered copy: attempts/001.csv, attempts/002.csv, ...
//
// results.csv, in this order. Lines that start with # are comments, so the size table reads as a plain CSV.
//   # version, student_id, date, cpu, os, compiler, flags, optimize/target/asm, warnings
//   # public_tests: <passed> of <total> pass
//   # test,name,result                       one line per public test; result is pass or fail (why)
//   m,n,p,time_s,...,result,ns_per_multiply_add   one row per size: HW1's columns, plus one.
//                                            ns_per_multiply_add = time_s / (m·n·p), in nanoseconds:
//                                            the average time of one c += a * b. Where it jumps between sizes, ask why.
//   # score_preview: <the cost formula>
//   # m,n,p,time_s,extra_memory_mb,abc_memory_mb,cost   one line per preview size (abc = A+B+C)
//   # geometric_mean: <the score preview>

